<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bayon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 12th–early 13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bayon is a Buddhist temple at the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, built as the state temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> in the late twelfth and early thirteenth centuries. Its towers carry some two hundred huge serene stone faces, and its bas-reliefs show battles against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> alongside scenes of markets, fishing and daily life. It is one of the most celebrated monuments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayon.jpg" alt="Bayon" width="320" height="144" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayon,_Angkor_Thom,_Camboya,_2013-08-17,_DD_37.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Bayon was the last state temple built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> and the only one built mainly for the worship of Buddhist deities, although many minor and local deities were also included as representatives of the districts and cities of the realm. It was the centrepiece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a>'s programme of monumental building and public works, which also produced the walls and nāga bridges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a> and the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ta-prohm">Ta Prohm</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banteay-kdei">Banteay Kdei</a>. Its original name was Jayagiri, and the name Bayon was given by Etienne Aymonier in 1880.</p><p>Later kings altered it. In the mid-13th century, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-viii">Jayavarman VIII</a>, the Khmer empire reverted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and the state temple was changed accordingly: sixteen chapels in the outer courtyard were demolished, and the principal image, a seated Buddha 3.6 m tall sheltered by the serpent king Mucalinda, was removed from the central sanctuary and smashed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> later became dominant, bringing further changes before the temple was abandoned to the jungle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The temple stands at the exact centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a>, with roads leading to it from the gates at each cardinal point, and it has no wall or moat of its own, using those of the city. It has two galleried enclosures and an upper terrace, crowded close together. The central tower rises 43 metres. The upper terrace carries the face towers, whose gigantic smiling faces are generally read as Avalokitesvara, the bodhisattva of compassion, or as portraits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a>; the two readings are not seen as exclusive. Local belief links the four faces with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a>. Towers have been added and lost over time: there were once 49, and 37 remain.</p><p>The outer gallery's bas-reliefs show a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> army on the march, a naval battle against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonle-sap">Tonle Sap</a>, markets, cooking, fishermen, a cockfight, palace scenes and a royal procession. No inscriptions accompany them, so which events are shown remains uncertain. The inner gallery, added under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-viii">Jayavarman VIII</a>, shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> scenes such as the Churning of the Sea of Milk.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In the first part of the 20th century the École Française d'Extrême Orient led the conservation of the temple and restored it by anastylosis. Since 1995 the Japanese Government Team for the Safeguarding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> has been the main conservation body. The Buddha figure was recovered from the bottom of a well in 1933, pieced together, and is now shown in a small pavilion at Angkor. Because its reliefs include everyday life in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a>, the Bayon is an important visual source for the study of Angkorian society.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bayon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>War elephants dominated the battlefield by their physical and psychological effect, while infantry fought with spears, swords and shields, as the reliefs of Angkor Wat and the <b>Bayon</b> depict.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Chams sacked Angkor in 1178, Jayavarman VII restored the empire, built a new capital at Angkor Thom with the <b>Bayon</b> as its state temple, and dedicated it to Buddhism, encouraged by his wife Indradevi, a devout Mahayana Buddhist.</blockquote><a href="/work/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>At its head stood a king whom the cult of the devaraja, the “god-king”, identified with Shiva or Vishnu, or under Jayavarman VII (r. 1181–1219) with the bodhisattva Avalokiteshvara, and whose temples, Angkor Wat and the <b>Bayon</b>, celebrated his divine rule on earth.</blockquote><a href="/societies/khmer-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Khmer Empire</a></li></ul></section>
